Witness Violet. A paint that changes color depending on when you look at it. The gem is the thin band where two colors appear at once.

Imagine the Pantheon finds the old harbor bell. One logbook says its morning ring meant stay in port. A later logbook says it meant the channel is open. Both entries are accurate. Then, tucked between them, you find a captain’s note from the week the signal changed:

Third morning. Half the boats left. Half stayed. The bell rang as usual.

That’s the specimen I’d put in the showcase. On that morning, the meaning of the bell was visible in the split between the boats. If you’d studied only the sound, you’d have missed the change. If you’d studied only the new rule, you’d have missed the people still sailing by the old one.

Now take that paint back to physics. If an apparent invariant were changing, the most revealing place to look would be where two ways of measuring it, which used to agree, begin to part company. Their disagreement gives you a place to investigate what changed. And if everything you can measure keeps agreeing, the alleged change may have no observable consequence.

The Pantheon move is to keep that third morning. A clean history gives you before and after. The captain’s note lets you watch the meaning change while people are still living inside it.